<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:conv="using:GalactiLog.App.Converters"
        xmlns:controls="using:GalactiLog.App.Controls"
        xmlns:cc="using:GalactiLog.App.ViewModels.CustomColumns"
        xmlns:detail="using:GalactiLog.App.ViewModels.TargetDetail"
        xmlns:vm="using:GalactiLog.App.ViewModels"
        xmlns:help="using:GalactiLog.Core.Help"
        xmlns:lvc="using:LiveChartsCore.SkiaSharpView.Avalonia">
  <!-- Control styles shared by more than one view. Included once from App.axaml, after
       FluentTheme, so a view that needs one of these classes uses it and defines nothing locally.
       The chevron began life in ActivityView.axaml; the moment the shell, the dashboard and the
       target list each needed the same flat glyph button it moved here rather than being pasted
       three more times.

       Phase 12 turns that habit into the rule. This file is the whole control vocabulary: the
       flat button default, the one filled button, the quiet and small buttons, the tag, the
       hairline rule, the four type tiers and the data-ink classes. A view that declares its own
       Button or Border.badge style is what ControlStyleScanTest fails on, because a convention
       spread across N views is forgotten at view N+1 (design lesson 2). -->
  <Styles.Resources>
    <!-- Scales.axaml's FontSize* keys are ratios to the root size, never point sizes, so a type
         tier reaches its size through this converter over the owning Window's FontSize. The
         setter form resolves (P12 R5's probe, FontSizeSetterProbeTest), which is why the four
         tiers below carry FontSize themselves and no call site repeats the attribute.

         The two above-root ratios are string literals because Scales.axaml holds no key above
         1.0 and MultiplyConverter parses a string; the two sub-root ones are the existing ratio
         keys, which still never touch a FontSize property directly. -->
    <conv:MultiplyConverter x:Key="Multiply" />

    <!-- Spec 12.12's help glyph: the ring and the mark the findings part once drew locally (that
         part went with Phase 24 R22), kept here where every view reaches them. -->
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconHelpRing">M1,8 a7,7 0 1,0 14,0 a7,7 0 1,0 -14,0</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconHelpMark">M8,14 v-5 M8,5.5 v-0.5</StreamGeometry>

    <!-- The help flyout's body, over one HelpTopic. It is a keyed template rather than a Setter on
         the Flyout property because a Setter's Template builds a Control and a Flyout is not one,
         and because a Flyout instance written straight into a Setter would be one object shared by
         every glyph in the application. HelpButton looks this key up and builds its own flyout
         from it, so the markup, the width and every token stay here.

         MaxWidth 420 is the readable measure at the shipped root size: the default text size is
         medium, which resolves to 16 px, whose average advance in the interface face is about
         8 px, so 420 px is roughly 52 characters per line, inside the 45 to 75 character band a
         five sentence paragraph needs. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="HelpTopicFlyoutTemplate" x:DataType="help:HelpTopic">
      <!-- Focusable, so spec 12.12's "the open flyout takes focus" is true rather than aspirational
           (Task 2A review P3). The body is two TextBlocks, neither of which can take focus, so with
           nothing focusable inside it FlyoutShowMode.Standard had nothing to move the keyboard to
           and it stayed on the glyph. IsTabStop stays off: this is a reading surface the flyout
           hands focus to on open, not a stop in the page's tab order. -->
      <StackPanel MaxWidth="420"
                  Spacing="6"
                  Focusable="True"
                  IsTabStop="False">
        <TextBlock Classes="t-label section" Text="{Binding Title}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        <TextBlock Classes="help-paragraph" Text="{Binding Paragraph}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- Spec 12.4 item 2 and ruling C4's rig label row, once. The session pane's filter table and
         its ranges table both draw it and both carried their own copy of these two runs, identical
         but for the column span the placing ContentControl now carries (phase review P3-3). The
         row model is one type as well, so the label and the "22 frames" sentence have one home.

         It is a keyed template rather than an implicit DataType template because the two tables
         place it inside their own row grids, where an implicit template would have to compete with
         the row templates already declared there. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="RigLabelRowTemplate" x:DataType="detail:RigLabelRowViewModel">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
        <TextBlock Classes="t-label section" Text="{Binding Label}" />
        <TextBlock Classes="t-label muted" Text="{Binding FrameCountText}" />
        <!-- Phase 20 Task 3: spec 12.15's rig-scope cells, as a trailing strip on the one rig label
             row both session pane tables draw. Every existing construction site builds an empty
             CellList, so a night that carries no rig-scope column draws exactly what it drew
             before this phase. IsVisible is bound rather than left to an empty ItemsControl: a
             visible zero-width strip still takes the StackPanel's own 8 pixel spacing, which is
             enough to move the pane's layout pins.

             Ruling C23 (fix pass 1): a rig line carries no header row, so a bare editor has no
             visible name. Each cell is preceded by its own column-name caption, Classes="t-label"
             alone (the secondary ink, not muted) at the same FontSizeLabel size FrameCountText
             already carries. -->
        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding CellList}" IsVisible="{Binding HasCells}">
          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="cc:CustomValueViewModel">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                <!-- The caption is decoration for a screen reader: the editor's own automation name
                     already begins with the column's name, so without this a reader hears the name
                     twice. The same AccessibilityView the LedgerRmsMarkCell dagger takes. -->
                <TextBlock Classes="t-label"
                           Text="{Binding Label}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" />
                <controls:CustomCellEditor />
              </StackPanel>
            </DataTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>
      </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- Phase 14B Task 6 appends this, and nothing above it changed. Spec 12.2's column gear
         (ruling D4, questions.md Q10): a ring with six radiating teeth, the same drawn-Path idiom
         IconHelpRing/IconHelpMark already use, rather than a Unicode character standing in for an
         icon (DESIGN.md section 8, spec 14.5). One StreamGeometry, kept inside this Resources
         block (the only place Avalonia accepts a bare resource in this file) because
         ControlStyleScanTest exempts only this file and a geometry is not a style; the beginning
         of the application-wide icon dictionary carried fixer item 26 asks for, and nothing
         else.

         Phase 14B fixer, fixer list item 42: the shape is RECORDED AS ACCEPTED rather than
         redrawn. The task6 review is right that six detached radial strokes around a ring read as
         brightness rather than as a gear at 16 px, and ToolTip.Tip="Columns" is what disambiguates
         it, which needs a hover. Redrawing it is an icon decision, not a fix pass decision, and
         14C's icon dictionary (carried item 26) is where the whole set is drawn to one hand. The
         note is here so that dictionary does not inherit this shape unexamined. -->
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconGear">
      M4,8 a4,4 0 1,0 8,0 a4,4 0 1,0 -8,0
      M12.3,8 L14.6,8 M10.2,11.7 L11.3,13.7 M5.8,11.7 L4.7,13.7
      M3.7,8 L1.4,8 M5.8,4.3 L4.7,2.3 M10.2,4.3 L11.3,2.3
    </StreamGeometry>

    <!-- Phase 14C Task 2 appends this, and nothing above it changed. Ruling E2: the collapsed
         vertical strip's item template, lifted out of Views/TargetDetail/TargetDetailView.axaml so
         the Dashboard filter panel's collapsed strip is the pattern's third use rather than its
         third hand copy (DESIGN.md section 6, design lesson 1).

         The template carries no ink. Each host supplies its own state colour through its own
         container selectors.

         x:DataType is the interface, which is legal for compiled bindings and is what lets one
         template serve SessionCardViewModel and FilterSectionViewModel at once. IsMonospace is
         bound rather than fixed because the ledger's dates are tabular ("09-08") and a filter
         section's short label ("Type") is not; one bound class is the whole difference. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="StripItemTemplate" x:DataType="vm:IStripItem">
      <!-- The Panel stretches with a transparent background so the whole cell, not the glyph
           strokes, is the tooltip's hit area. -->
      <Panel Background="Transparent" ToolTip.Tip="{Binding FullLabel}">
        <TextBlock Classes="t-label"
                   Classes.mono="{Binding IsMonospace}"
                   Text="{Binding ShortLabel}"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   IsVisible="{Binding IconKey, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNull}}" />
        <Path Classes="icon-glyph"
              Width="16"
              Height="16"
              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
              Data="{Binding IconKey, Converter={x:Static conv:IconConverters.KeyToGeometry}}"
              IsVisible="{Binding IconKey, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
      </Panel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- The chevron pair, moved here from TargetDetailView.axaml in the same window. The dashboard
         needs a drawn chevron for both of its collapse affordances, DESIGN.md section 8 refuses a
         Unicode glyph standing in for an icon, and a fourth copy of the pair is exactly the copy
         design lesson 1 forbids at the pattern's third use. -->
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconChevronLeft">M10,3 L5,8 L10,13</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconChevronRight">M6,3 L11,8 L6,13</StreamGeometry>

    <!-- Polish wave 1 ruling 6: the two collapsed 48 px strips draw these instead of text. Rail
         first, then the dashboard filter sections; Settings reuses IconGear. -->
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconDashboard">M2,2 h5 v5 h-5 z M9,2 h5 v5 h-5 z M2,9 h5 v5 h-5 z M9,9 h5 v5 h-5 z</StreamGeometry>
    <!-- Spec 12.17's Mosaics rail entry: two overlapping panels. -->
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconMosaics">M1.5,2.5 h8 v6 h-8 z M6.5,7.5 h8 v6 h-8 z</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconStatistics">M2,9 h3 v5 h-3 z M6.5,2 h3 v12 h-3 z M11,5.5 h3 v8.5 h-3 z</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconAnalysis">M2,2 V14 H14 M4.5,11 C6.5,11 7,5 9.5,6 S12.5,3.5 14,3</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconActivity">M1,8 a7,7 0 1,0 14,0 a7,7 0 1,0 -14,0 M8,4.5 V8 L10.5,9.5</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconDiagnostics">M1,8 a7,7 0 1,0 14,0 a7,7 0 1,0 -14,0 M3.5,8 H6 L7.5,5.5 L9,10.5 L10.5,8 H12.5</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconSearch">M2,7 a5,5 0 1,0 10,0 a5,5 0 1,0 -10,0 M10.5,10.5 L14.5,14.5</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconObjectType">M8,2 L9.76,6.07 L14.18,6.49 L10.85,9.43 L11.82,13.76 L8,11.5 L4.18,13.76 L5.15,9.43 L1.82,6.49 L6.24,6.07 Z</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconDateRange">M2,4 h12 v10 h-12 z M2,7.5 h12 M5,2 v3 M11,2 v3</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconFilters">M2,3 H14 L9.5,8.5 V13.5 L6.5,12 V8.5 Z</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconEquipment">M1.5,5.5 h3 l1.5,-2 h4 l1.5,2 h3 v8 h-13 z M5.5,9 a2.5,2.5 0 1,0 5,0 a2.5,2.5 0 1,0 -5,0</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconMetrics">M1.5,12 A6.5,6.5 0 0 1 14.5,12 M8,12 L11.5,7.5</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconHeaderQuery">M3,1.5 h7 l3,3 v10 h-10 z M10,1.5 v3 h3 M5.5,8 h5 M5.5,11 h5</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconCustom">M2,4 H14 M2,8 H14 M2,12 H14 M3.5,4 a1.5,1.5 0 1,0 3,0 a1.5,1.5 0 1,0 -3,0 M9.5,8 a1.5,1.5 0 1,0 3,0 a1.5,1.5 0 1,0 -3,0 M5.5,12 a1.5,1.5 0 1,0 3,0 a1.5,1.5 0 1,0 -3,0</StreamGeometry>

    <!-- Spec 14's thin scrollbar. Ruling E3 is REVERSED (phase-review P1 and P2, fixer-list item
         1): the hand-written ScrollBar control theme this file carried is withdrawn and the
         platform Fluent theme is kept, restyled through the resource keys it already reads.

         The hand-written template cost an inverted axis and a lost state in one phase. Its Track
         declared IsDirectionReversed="False" for both orientations, and Avalonia's Track, like
         WPF's, maps an increase in y to a decrease in value, so every vertical bar in the
         application rendered upside down: thumb at the bottom at offset zero, dragging down
         scrolled up. It also replaced Fluent's collapsed and expanded states, so a full strength
         thumb was drawn permanently over the trailing content of every scroller. Neither was
         visible to the suite. Restyling the platform theme keeps the direction, the paging, the
         line buttons and the two states by construction; only the sizes and the inks are ours.

         The keys are the ones Avalonia 11.3.21's src/Avalonia.Themes.Fluent/Controls/ScrollBar.xaml
         reads, with their defaults in Accents/FluentControlResources.xaml. ScrollBarSize is the
         bar's width in either orientation (16 by default, 8 here). The six brushes are aliased
         beside the tokens themselves, in each of the three theme dictionaries under Theme/Themes:
         a resource declared here cannot follow a theme swap, because a ResourceDictionary entry is
         a constructed object and StaticResource in this file would freeze on whichever dictionary
         App.axaml merged at parse time. The dictionaries carry the alias the same way they already
         carry the eight callout composites, as a StaticResource to the token beside it, so the
         swap moves the whole set at once and the three tokens stay the one place a colour is
         written.

         What ships, and what spec 14 and DESIGN.md section 6 now say: at rest the bar is Fluent's
         hairline panning thumb in ColorScrollbarThumb over a transparent track; with the pointer
         over it the bar expands to the full 8 pixels with its two line buttons, the thumb taking
         ColorScrollbarThumbHover under the pointer and while dragging. There is no third colour:
         the pressed fill is aliased to the hover token, which is the "no separate pressed state"
         rule 14 took from the web, kept through a theme that does declare a pressed rule. The bar
         still overlays its scroller and takes no layout space, which is the Fluent ScrollViewer
         theme's own AllowAutoHide behaviour and was never this block's doing. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="ScrollBarSize">8</x:Double>

    <!-- Spec 12.16, ruling B5: the target page's two send submenus bind ItemsSource to
         TargetDetailViewModel.NinaSendItems / StellariumSendItems, and this theme is what puts
         Header, Command, IsEnabled and the tooltip on each generated MenuItem, which an
         ItemTemplate cannot reach. It lives here rather than in the view because a control theme
         is shared vocabulary, which ControlStyleScanTest now enforces (ruling B30). -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="IntegrationSendItemTheme"
                  TargetType="MenuItem"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}"
                  x:DataType="detail:IntegrationSendItemViewModel">
      <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Name}" />
      <Setter Property="Command" Value="{Binding SendCommand}" />
      <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding IsEnabled}" />
      <Setter Property="ToolTip.Tip" Value="{Binding ToolTipText}" />
    </ControlTheme>
  </Styles.Resources>

  <!-- Spec 13's four tooltip and legend paints, on every chart control in the process, which
       corrects a shipped defect.

       ChartTheme.Apply writes these four paints into LiveCharts.DefaultSettings, and the Avalonia
       chart controls resolve those four styled-property DEFAULTS once per process, from whatever
       the settings held when the FIRST chart control in the process was built. The settings track
       a theme swap correctly and no control ever re-read them, so a user who changed the theme in
       Settings got re-coloured axes, series and pills and a tooltip and legend still painted in
       the theme the application started in. Both a chart alive across the swap and a chart built
       afterwards were wrong.

       A Setter beats a styled-property default, which is the whole defect, and a DynamicResource
       re-evaluates when the resource is written, which is the whole reassignment: ChartTheme.Apply
       writes these four keys into Application.Current.Resources at the end of every call. So this
       block is the choke point, and a chart control is painted by construction rather than by each
       view remembering an attribute (design lesson 2). It covers the six charts the Statistics
       page builds from one DataTemplate and every chart a later phase adds.

       All three chart types the package ships are selected, because a pie tooltip is painted from
       the same four properties as a cartesian one and PolarChart is here so the third type cannot
       be the one that is forgotten. ChartPaintCensusTests holds both halves: no chart element in
       the markup may set one of the four locally, since a local value beats this Setter, and this
       block must declare all three types with all four paints.

       ControlStyleScanTest does not see this block: its Button rule extracts only selectors
       beginning Button or ToggleButton, and its view scan skips this file outright and carries no
       lvc needle. No exclusion was added to it.

       Three blocks rather than one comma-separated selector, and that is a compiler rule and not a
       preference: Avalonia's XAML compiler resolves a Setter's target type from the selector, and
       a multi-type selector resolves to the common base, which is UserControl here. The four
       properties are declared on each chart type and not on UserControl, so the one-block form
       fails to build with AVLN2000 on all four setters. Measured, not assumed. -->
  <Style Selector="lvc|CartesianChart">
    <Setter Property="TooltipBackgroundPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartTooltipBackgroundPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="TooltipTextPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartTooltipTextPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="LegendBackgroundPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartLegendBackgroundPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="LegendTextPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartLegendTextPaint}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="lvc|PieChart">
    <Setter Property="TooltipBackgroundPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartTooltipBackgroundPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="TooltipTextPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartTooltipTextPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="LegendBackgroundPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartLegendBackgroundPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="LegendTextPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartLegendTextPaint}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- No PolarChart ships today. The selector is here because the census asserts all three types
       the package declares these four properties on, so the chart kind a later phase reaches for
       is painted the day it is written rather than being the one that is forgotten. -->
  <Style Selector="lvc|PolarChart">
    <Setter Property="TooltipBackgroundPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartTooltipBackgroundPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="TooltipTextPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartTooltipTextPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="LegendBackgroundPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartLegendBackgroundPaint}" />
    <Setter Property="LegendTextPaint" Value="{DynamicResource ChartLegendTextPaint}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- P12 R8's interface face and the application's default text ink, applied at the one place
       every window inherits from, so a sixth dialog cannot forget either. FontFamily and Foreground
       are both inherited properties, so the per-site attributes and the text classes already in the
       views keep winning locally: inheritance is the lowest priority there is, and anything that
       sets its own Foreground, a button's content, a badge, a callout, a caption, is untouched.

       The ink is declared HERE and not as a Selector="TextBlock" rule: a rule on the type would
       outrank the ink a control gives its own content, and the one filled button in the application
       would draw its label in the page ink over its accent fill. Until this setter landed, nothing
       in the application declared a default at all, so every TextBlock with neither a class that
       sets Foreground nor an attribute of its own, 99 of them in the shipped views, inherited
       FluentTheme's own Dark default, pure white, a colour no GalactiLog dictionary declares. On
       red-light, the night-vision theme, the Analysis stats card figures and every other tabular
       figure in the application were the brightest thing on the screen. DynamicResource and never
       Static: the ink follows the Settings theme picker. InheritedInkTests is the pin. -->
  <Style Selector="Window">
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontSans}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The flat button default. This replaces Fluent's filled ButtonBackground with a 1 px outline
       over a transparent fill, which is the direction's whole control story and the reason
       ControlStyleScanTest can assert that no Button style carries a gradient or a tinted fill.

       CornerRadius 3 is a literal rather than RadiusSm (6) on purpose: the approved comp's
       controls are 3 px, and spec 14.4's radius scale is theme-independent and must not be
       redeclared per theme, so this is a control decision inside one style file rather than a new
       token. Task 7 records it in spec 14.4. -->
  <Style Selector="Button">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderEmphasis}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="3" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,0" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="30" />
    <!-- Fluent's Button theme sets no VerticalContentAlignment and the ContentControl default is
         Stretch, so a stretched label draws its line at the top of the 30 px box once the
         11,5,11,6 Fluent padding above is replaced by 12,0. -->
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="3" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgHover}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderEmphasis}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgElevated}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- P12 R7's single filled control: Run scan in the status bar, and nothing else. The fill is
       ColorAccent at full strength with the surface colour as its ink, which is the only place in
       the application where an accent is a fill rather than a 1 px edge. Spec 14.5's "buttons
       never use a solid accent fill" is amended by Task 7 to name this one exception. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.primary /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
    <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgSurface}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.primary">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.primary:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccentHover}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The two state blocks the filled button needs, and why they are here rather than inherited.
       The base Button:pressed and Button:disabled blocks are declared above the unconditional
       Button.primary presenter setters, and both sides carry a selector activator, so the later
       declaration wins while both are active: without these two blocks a disabled Run scan keeps
       the full strength accent fill and renders identically to its enabled self, and a pressed one
       shows the hover fill and no feedback at all. Declaring them after the .primary rest and
       hover blocks makes the intended value win by declaration rather than by accident.
       ButtonStateStyleTest asserts the resulting presenter brushes rather than this reasoning.

       Disabled is the comp's flat form (comp-observing-ledger.html:258): no fill, the 1 px
       emphasis outline (rgba(255,255,255,0.15), which is ColorBorderEmphasis #26FFFFFF) and
       tertiary ink. The state is reachable in the shipped application: StatusBarViewModel's
       CanRunScan is false while a name resolution holds the lease and the button stays visible.
       Background is the literal Transparent because that is what the base Button:disabled block
       above uses for the same "no fill" and there is no fill token for it. The setters sit on the
       presenter because Fluent's Button template root is the presenter and template binds its
       Background, BorderBrush and CornerRadius from the Button. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.primary:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderEmphasis}" />
    <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The pressed fill is a rung of its own on the accent ramp, below rest, rather than a reuse of
       the rest value. A keyboard activation raises :pressed without :pointerover, so with a
       two token ramp (rest and hover) a space bar press on Run scan changed nothing at all and the
       control gave the keyboard user no feedback (Task 1 fix pass 2 escalation, coordinator
       item 2). ColorAccentPressed is the third rung and is declared in all three dictionaries. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.primary:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccentPressed}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The quiet text button (Back, Details, the band toggles). -->
  <Style Selector="Button.quiet">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The small button (the frame table toolbar, the pane's Notes toggle). -->
  <Style Selector="Button.sm">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,0" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="Button.chevron">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,0" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.chevron:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgHover}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A badge is outlined text in tertiary ink, never a filled pill: ColorBadgeBg is transparent
       in every dictionary and this outline is what makes it legible. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tag">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBadgeBg}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderDefault}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="3" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,1" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tag TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The hairline rule. One bottom edge, one border token, no card. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.rule">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderDefault}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,1" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The four type tiers (spec 14.4, P12 R5). Ratios to the owning Window's root size:
       t-hero 1.5, t-stat 1.2, t-label 0.786, t-caption 0.714. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.t-hero">
    <Setter Property="FontSize"
            Value="{Binding $parent[Window].FontSize,
                            Converter={StaticResource Multiply},
                            ConverterParameter=1.5}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.t-stat">
    <Setter Property="FontSize"
            Value="{Binding $parent[Window].FontSize,
                            Converter={StaticResource Multiply},
                            ConverterParameter=1.2}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.t-label">
    <Setter Property="FontSize"
            Value="{Binding $parent[Window].FontSize,
                            Converter={StaticResource Multiply},
                            ConverterParameter={StaticResource FontSizeLabel}}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.t-caption">
    <Setter Property="FontSize"
            Value="{Binding $parent[Window].FontSize,
                            Converter={StaticResource Multiply},
                            ConverterParameter={StaticResource FontSizeCaption}}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The log line's figures stay at root size and take weight 600 instead, which is the fifth
       and last tier the comp uses. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.figure">
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="FontFeatures" Value="tnum=1" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Three data-ink classes the direction needs everywhere. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.num">
    <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Right" />
    <Setter Property="FontFeatures" Value="tnum=1" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.mono">
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontMono}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The one "worse" ink. Colour is spent only on data; this is the single semantic reading on
       the page and it is never a fill, never a left edge and never a pill (P12 direction). -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.worse">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorMetricWorst}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Section labels (Nights, Frames). Sentence case, weight 600, label tier, no tracking: the
       inputs' type table removes letter-spacing from this page entirely. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.section">
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- P12 Task 5 appends everything below, and nothing above it changed.

       The toggle pill (the two chart pill strips, P12 R9). A ToggleButton is not a Button, so the
       flat default at the top of this file does not reach it and Fluent's own filled toggle would
       have shown through. Same flat shape: a transparent fill, a 1 px outline, and the elevated
       field when checked, which is the direction's rule that light carries state while colour
       carries data. The pill's own colour is the 7 px dot its template puts beside the label, and
       that dot is user data (a filter's configured colour) or a fixed metric token. -->
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderDefault}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="3" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,3" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="3" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgHover}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton:checked /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgElevated}" />
    <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton:checked">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderEmphasis}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The collapsible section (P24 R18): every Expander in the application, Diagnostics, the
       dashboard filters, the Display groups and the target page's sections. The pill block above
       reaches the Expander's own header toggle, so without this the header wore the pill's outline
       at rest and its elevated fill when expanded, and Fluent's own hover fill under the pointer.
       Here the header is bare at rest and when open, the one hover tint is ColorBgHover on the
       header's background border (the pill's presenter tint is cleared so the two never stack),
       the content border is invisible, and the chevron and the platform focus adorner are
       untouched. Declared after the pill block because both sides carry activators and the later
       declaration wins. -->
  <Style Selector="Expander">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Expander /template/ ToggleButton#ExpanderHeader">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Expander /template/ ToggleButton#ExpanderHeader:checked /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Expander /template/ ToggleButton#ExpanderHeader:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Expander /template/ ToggleButton#ExpanderHeader:pointerover /template/ Border#ToggleButtonBackground">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgHover}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Expander /template/ ToggleButton#ExpanderHeader:pressed /template/ Border#ToggleButtonBackground">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgHover}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The selectable pill (the activity severity and category filters, the equipment and filter
       suggestion pills). One definition, lifted here from the three views that each carried their
       own copy: ActivityView.axaml declared the shape and EquipmentTabView.axaml and
       FiltersTabView.axaml each re-declared half of it, which is the pattern design lesson 1 says
       to end at the second occurrence rather than the fourth. The shape is the direction's flat
       one: no filled rest state, no accent fill when selected, the same lit idiom as every other
       selected thing in the application.

       The selected background sits on the presenter rather than on the Button, because Fluent's
       template binds the presenter's Background from the Button and a :pointerover presenter rule
       would otherwise win over a Button level setter. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.pill">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,2" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,4,0" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderDefault}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Normal" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.pill.selected">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderEmphasis}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.pill.selected /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgElevated}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.pill.selected:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgHover}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The frame table's header cell: a sort button that reads from its left edge over a text
       column and over its figures on a numeric one (spec 14.4). Two alignment setters and no
       chrome, lifted out of Views/TargetDetail/FrameTableView.axaml so the choke-point rule stays
       literal: no view under src/ declares a Button style, with no exemptions to remember. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.header-cell">
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.header-cell.numeric">
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Right" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Spec 14.5's 20 and 50 percent callout composite. Lifted here from the two views that each
       carried their own copy, which is design lesson 1's second-occurrence rule and the same
       breach the three Button.pill copies above were. No left edge: the direction refuses a
       coloured edge above 1 px and a callout's whole border is that 1 px.

       The warn arm carries the warning ink for its text as well, because the pane's callouts
       relied on that descendant rule while the page's set it inline on each TextBlock; an inline
       value is a LocalValue and still wins, so both readings survive the lift. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.callout">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="3" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,4" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.callout.warn">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorWarningCalloutFill}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorWarningCalloutBorder}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.callout.warn TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorWarning}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.callout.error">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorErrorCalloutFill}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorErrorCalloutBorder}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Phase 14A Task 2 appends everything below, and nothing above it changed.

       Spec 12.12's help glyph, Controls/HelpButton.cs. The selector is Button.help rather than
       controls|HelpButton because HelpButton overrides StyleKeyOverride to typeof(Button): a
       control deriving from a templated control finds no ControlTheme under its own type, so
       without that override the glyph would have no template and would render nothing. A type
       selector matches on the style key, so with the override in place a controls|HelpButton
       selector can never match and Button.help always does. The class is added by the control
       itself, so no call site can forget it. This file is the one place ControlStyleScanTest
       allows a Button style to be declared at all.

       The shape is Button.chevron's: padding 6,0, no border, tertiary ink, primary ink on hover.
       The control adds that class to itself alongside help rather than this style restating its
       four setters, so the two cannot drift (Task 2A review P3); what is left below is only what a
       help glyph adds to a chevron. MinHeight is cleared because the flat button default is 30 px
       tall and this glyph sits inside a heading's own row in twenty five views, which it must not
       inflate. The width is fixed so the target page's identity line, a DockPanel where every
       child has to trim, cannot pick the glyph as the element that trims. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.help">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="28" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Content">
      <Template>
        <Panel Width="16" Height="16">
          <Path Classes="help-glyph" Data="{StaticResource IconHelpRing}" />
          <Path Classes="help-glyph" Data="{StaticResource IconHelpMark}" />
        </Panel>
      </Template>
    </Setter>
  </Style>
  <!-- Phase 14B fixer, fixer list item 41: the four stroke setters serve every drawn icon in the
       vocabulary, not only the help glyph. The dashboard's column gear repeated all four inline
       and got no pointer-over ink, which made it the one drawn icon that did not brighten on
       hover. One declaration over both class names rather than a fourth copy of the setters
       (design-lessons rule 1); icon-glyph is the name a later icon reaches for, and help-glyph
       stays because two views and the help button template already spell it. -->
  <Style Selector="Path.help-glyph, Path.icon-glyph">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
    <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="1.4" />
    <Setter Property="StrokeLineCap" Value="Round" />
    <Setter Property="StrokeJoin" Value="Round" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.help:pointerover Path.help-glyph">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button:pointerover Path.icon-glyph">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.help-paragraph">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Spec 12.4's per-frame quality grading (PAR-003), appended by Phase 14A Task 3. The band is
       carried by the cell's ink alone: no cell and no row takes a tinted fill (ruling C3, and
       DESIGN.md's refusal list). There is no band-neutral class, because spec 12.4's band table
       gives neutral ColorTextPrimary, "which is to say no mark".

       The spec's band table names the colours ColorSuccessValue, ColorWarningValue and
       ColorErrorValue. Those keys are Color resources; a Foreground and a Background bind a brush,
       so these bind ColorSuccess, ColorWarning and ColorError, which are the SolidColorBrush
       resources built from exactly those three colours. The spec names the colour and the markup
       binds its brush, and they are the same token (questions.md Q14).

       This block sits after TextBlock.worse (line 256) deliberately, and moving it above would
       change what the application draws. Avalonia resolves equal-specificity selectors by
       declaration order, so on an element carrying both worse and a band class the band wins here
       and the worse ink wins if the order is reversed. The surface that depends on it is the
       preview modal's metadata badges (Task 7), where both classes are set from the view model and
       both styles are application level; the frame table's own cells stack the same two classes
       but resolve them in that view's own style host, which is applied last. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.band-better">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorSuccess}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.band-watch">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorWarning}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.band-reject">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorError}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Phase 14C Task 2 appends these two, and nothing above them changed. One item of a collapsed
       vertical strip: the Dashboard filter panel's strip draws them today and a later strip that
       is a request rather than a selection draws the same pair.

       Geometry only, plus DESIGN.md section 6's "the open or active item in the accent ink". No
       fill of its own: Button.quiet already carries the transparent surface and the borderless
       form, and the hover light comes from the base Button. The strip is 48 pixels wide, so the
       padding is vertical alone. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.strip-item">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0,5" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.strip-item.active TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.strip-item.active Path.icon-glyph">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Phase 14C Task 5 appends everything below, and nothing above it changed. -->

  <!-- Q11 reversed, fix pass: a shared ScrollViewer style setting AllowAutoHide="False" stood
       here, to reserve every scroller's gutter. It reached only the 27 markup ScrollViewers
       (a bare ScrollViewer selector matches the logical tree, not the ScrollViewer a ListBox or a
       ComboBox popup builds inside its own template) and, on the ones it did reach, Avalonia
       reserves the bar's layout space only once a bar can actually be drawn, so content still
       narrowed by 8 pixels the moment one appeared rather than never shifting, which was the
       clause's whole point. The session pane paid for this in three places at once before it was
       withdrawn. Every scroller now keeps Avalonia's default overlay behaviour, the same as
       before this phase: no layout space taken, so nothing to reserve and nothing to narrow.

       A control that sits at a scroller's trailing edge, outside the scroller, keeps its own 8
       pixels clear of the overlaid bar by its own margin or padding if it needs to; nothing here
       does that for it. -->

  <!-- The ScrollBar hover state that used to sit here is withdrawn with the control theme it
       belonged to (fixer-list item 1). It named Thumb#thumb, a part of the hand-written template,
       and the hover ink is now the ScrollBarThumbFillPointerOver alias the Fluent thumb theme
       already reads. No ScrollBar style ships at all; ControlStyleScanTest's two ScrollBar needles
       stay, because what they guard is a view declaring one of its own. -->

  <!-- Carried fixer-list item 32: MainWindow.axaml's navigation rail and SettingsView.axaml's tab
       strip each declared a five-rule ListBoxItem style block, byte-identical, comment included,
       except for one setter value (padding 16,10 against 14,8). Classes="rail" cannot go on a
       ListBoxItem itself: its containers are generated by the ListBox and Avalonia has no Classes
       setter, so the fold is class selectors on the two ListBoxes instead. Six selectors replace
       ten and nothing renders differently, which is the point: a fold that also restyles a page
       is two changes reported as one.

       Foreground is set by a style rather than on the TextBlock itself, so the selected-state
       setter below is not outranked by a local value (the comment both former copies carried,
       kept with the moved rules because it is the reason the shape is what it is). -->
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail ListBoxItem">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,10" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail ListBoxItem TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail ListBoxItem:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgHover}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail ListBoxItem:selected /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgElevated}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail ListBoxItem:selected TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail ListBoxItem Path.icon-glyph">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail ListBoxItem:selected Path.icon-glyph">
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail.compact ListBoxItem">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,8" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The collapsed navigation rail: the item's title rides the ListBoxItem as its tip, so the whole
       48 px entry answers a hover; the expanded rail shows the title and the tip goes with the class. -->
  <Style Selector="ListBox.rail.collapsed ListBoxItem" x:DataType="vm:NavigationItem">
    <Setter Property="ToolTip.Tip" Value="{Binding Title}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Phase 14C Task 4 appends everything below, and nothing above it changed. -->

  <!-- The pager's current-page button (spec 12.2). Button.sm already gives every page button its
       row height; Button.page.current is the one class the current page carries over that, the
       same shape Button.pill.selected above already uses: the emphasis border, primary ink and an
       elevated presenter, so the current page reads as lit rather than growing a second button
       style (task4.md section 6, coordinator ruling 1). -->
  <Style Selector="Button.page.current">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBorderEmphasis}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.page.current /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ColorBgElevated}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The refetch dim (spec 12.2, "The refetch dim"): while a filtered query is in flight, the
       rows already on screen stay rendered and dim, matching the web's opacity-50
       pointer-events-none treatment of the same interval (TargetFeed.tsx). On the rows
       ItemsControl's own class, never inside a Button or ToggleButton style block:
       ControlStyleScanTest.NoButtonStyle_CarriesAGradientOrASemiTransparentAccentFill forbids any
       Opacity setter inside one of those, and this selector is neither. The input-disable half is
       TargetListView.axaml's own IsHitTestVisible binding (fix pass, task4-review.md P3: an
       IsEnabled subtree drops keyboard focus in Avalonia and does not restore it, which the
       refetch dim must not do), which this file has no part of. -->
  <Style Selector="ItemsControl.refetching">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.5" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Phase 20 Task 3 appends everything below, and nothing above it changed. -->

  <!-- Spec 12.15's refused cell: a custom column value the repository would not write keeps the
       stored value on screen, takes the error ink, and carries the refusal sentence on its tooltip
       and its AutomationProperties.HelpText until the next successful write. Nothing opens a
       dialog and nothing pushes a toast, so this ink is the whole of the refusal on a table row.

       Three selectors on one style rather than a class on a wrapper, because the Foreground and the
       BorderBrush belong on the editor itself and Controls/CustomCellEditor.axaml declares no style
       of its own. None of ControlStyleScanTest's seven needles matches these three selectors, and
       the walk skips this file outright, which is why the style is legal here and nowhere else. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBox.refused, CheckBox.refused, ComboBox.refused">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource ColorError}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource ColorError}" />
  </Style>
</Styles>
